Installation:
=============

Copy the file garbagecollector.library to Libs:
If you extracted with the x command of LhA all files are extracted to their
right directory. I estamate that you use something like:
LHA x Userlst1.lha RAM:
All stuff in RAM:BBS/Bin has to go to your BBS:Bin, RAM:BBS/Configs to your
BBS:Configs and so on... Be careful with the file BBS/Configs/Main.Rx. This
file  should  not  be  copied  to  your  BBS:Configs, if you already have a
Main.Rx  in  there.  If you already have a Main.Rx then edit it and put the
content  of  the  supplied  Main.Rx  in  there. Edit key and access to your
needs.  You  have  to  disable the original Userlist command of TransAmiga:
Edit  BBS:Configs/Main.cmds  and give the userlist command a key that never
will  be  used and access 256 so nobody can use it (even to the SysOp :-) )
An example Main.cmds is supplied with this archive. I just took the example
Tim  included  with the original TransAmiga archive and edited the Userlist
entry.  Examine  the  examples and you will see how it works. FIRST compare
the  files  of this archive to your own files then edit or copy! As I think
it  is not the first time you insall an external program in TransAmiga I do
not  go  in  detail. If you need more help contact me or a TransAmiga SysOp
who  can  help you. If you are not experienced then backup your BBS:Configs
before copying or editing anything.


                     The basic command that works most
                    ==================================
                    ==================================

Commandlineusage:
=================

Userlist [-C<configfile>] [-O<outputfile>] [-S<sortierkriterium>]
         [-U<userfileverzeichnis] [-L<leaveoutfile>]

All  options in brackets [] are optional. They can be left out. If left out
default values are used.

Description of parameters:
==========================

-C<configfile>
--------------

This  is not a configfile that replaces the commandline options! It defines
the outfit of the userlist.
Every  line only one command! All commands are case insensitive: NaMe(5) is
interpreted as NAME(5) like namE(5) is.

a  in  brackets  represents a number. The number indicates that the command
shall produce output that has the length 'a'. If the data does not fill the
whole length spaces are added at the right side to reach 'a' characters.

b in brackets also represents a number. This time the number indicates how
many digits the output of the command shall have. You see this is for
numbers. '0' is added at the left side tf the digits of the number don't
reach the length of 'b'. If 'b' is too small so that the number does not
fit into the field only the right part of the number is displayed.

c is something special. It just can be 1 or 2. All commands that have c in
brackets display a date. 1 produces dd.mm.yy, 2 produces mm/dd/yy
dd=day, mm=month, yy=year



NAME(a)

Is  replaced by the name of the user. The user name has a maximum length of
20  characters  (TransAmiga  restriction).  a  tells  the  command how many
characters the output shall be. If 'a' is shorter than the name of the user
the leftmost 'a' characters are displayed. If the length of name is shorter
than  'a' spaces are put on the right side of the name. a is not limited to
20 you can use a higher value to have some spaces between the names and the
following output.

STREET(a)

The same thing as NAME(a) only the street address is put in. TransAmiga's
maximum length for this is 80 characters.

CITY(a)

Same as STREET(a) - only the city is put in.

STATE(a)

Same as STREET(a) - only the state is put in.

PHONE(a)

Same as STREET(a) - only the phone number is put in.

COMPUTER(a)

Same as STREET(a) - only the computer is put in.

POSTAL(a)

Same as STREET(a) - only the postal code is put in.

HANDLE(a)

Same  as  NAME(a) - only the handle is put in. This option only makes sense
if handles are allowed at your board.

PASSWORD(a)

Same  as  NAME(a) - only  the  password  is  put  in. The maximum length of
passwords  is  10.  Be carefull with this option! It normally should not be
used to protect your users!

KDOWN(b)

The  downloaded  kbyte are displayed. b indicates the number of digits that
shall  be displayed. If the number has less digits than specified 0 will be
put  at  the  left  side  of the output. If the number has more digits than
specified  the  leftmost  digits  are  cut off and lost. Example: If a user
downloaded  5012 k  and  you  put b=2 the output will be 12. So think twice
about the value of b. A little experimentation will help here. :-)

FILEDOWN(b)

Same as KDOWN(b) - only the number of downloaded files is displayed.

KUP(b)

Same as KDOWN(b) - only the uploaded kbyte are displayed.

FULEUP(b)

Same as KDOWN(b) - only the number of uploaded files is displayed.

MESSAGES(b)

Same as KDOWN(b) - only  the  number  of  messages  the user has written is
displayed.

CALLS(b)

Same as KDOWN(b) - only the number of the user's calls is displayed.

SCRLENGTH(b)

Same as KDOWN(b) - only the user's screen length is displayed. TransAmiga's
maximum is 60 here so it is safe if you put b=2.

USERNUMBER(b)

Same as KDOWN(b) - only the usernumber is displayed.

LASTCALL(c)

This is the date of the user's last call. It covers always 8 characters.
You can use 1 or 2 as a value for c (discribed above).

BIRTH(c)

Same as LASTCALL - only the user's birth date is displayed.

RATIO

This  and  the  following commands have no parameter as it doesn't make any
sense.   The   output   follows   a  special  pattern.  In  this  case  the
upload:download ratio is displayed as xxx:1 covering 5 characters.

DAYLIMIT

User's  dayly  timelimit.  The  highest possible value is 255 so the output
always covers 3 characters.

TIMELEFT

This  dislpays  the  time  the  user  had when he loggef off last. Covers 3
characters. I don't think that this command is of any use but perhaps there
is someone out there who really loves this feature...?

COMMANDMODE

This  is the command mode the user uses. The output is "CommandStacking" or
"    Hot-Keys   " (without quotes). It covers 15 characters.

GRAPHMODE

This is the graphic mode the user has activated. Possible output is "ASCII"
or "ANSI " (again without quotes). Covers 5 characters.

EDITORTYPE

This   is   the  type  of  editor  the  user  specified:  "Line-Editor"  or
"Full-Screen". Always 11 characters.

ACCESS

This is the access level of the user. Output is 3 characters as the maximum
is 255.

NETMAIL

This is the netmail credit. Again 3 characters, again the maximum is 255.

FLAGS

The user's flags are displayed. Not as a number as it is stored in the user
files. It covers 8 characters and looks like X----X--

HELPLEVEL

The  help  level  the  user  has.  Output  is  Novice  or  Expert, covers 6
characters.  If  a user has novice mode the menu is displayed always, if he
has Expert only the menu prompt is displayed in TransAmiga.

SPACE(a)

This  is  a  very  helpfull  command. It does not give any information from
TransAmiga,  it just puts 'a' spaces in. If 'a' is 5 you will have 5 spaces
in the output.

CRLF

This and the following command define the end of line (EOL). You can put it
between  any  commands you want. ATTENTION: One of these commands has to be
the  last command in the config file otherwise your line will become longer
and  longer. If it becomes longer than 255 some info is lost as the maximum
length  of  a  line  is 255. I suggest a maximum length of 80 characters as
this is the linelength most terminals have.
CRLF  puts  a carriage return (CR) and a line feed (LF) in. This is the EOL
code of IBM and clones. TransAmiga understands it and displays it right. If
you make the userlist available for download you should use this command as
users with IBM can type it. If you only have Amiga users you will use LF as
the EOL code of Amigas is LF. TransAmiga displays everything right as it
detects both EOL.

LF

Read the description of CRLF.

LF  and  CRLF put a EOL in. This can be used to produce more than one line
output  for  every user. Just put in more than one of these commands. Play
around with this option and you will soon discover the possebilities. :-)

                                     |
                                   --+--
                                     |

How  can  I  make  a nice-looking userlist? It's very simple. Use the above
commands  to  define  the  output.  Put  a  SPACE(1) or something like that
between  the commands to have some space between the different information.
To  go to next line just put a LF or CRLF in there. Don't forget do put one
in the last line of th econfig file! Examine the examples I included in the
configs  directory and play a bit with them. If you do not use the examples
don't forget to change the ARexx scripts.

ANSI?  No  problem. You can include ANSI-Codes in the config file. Make two
config files: one with ANSI codes and one without them. Now you can produce
ANSI and NONANSI userlists. User the ANSI introducer (character 27) NOT the
Amiga-CSI-Introducer  (it  is  actually  not discoverd and the line will be
ignored). Have a look at the supplied UserlistANSI.cfg

All  lines that don't start with an ANSI-Code or command are ignored. So be
sure  not  to  put  spaces in front of your commands and write the commands
correctly or you will miss some information :-) AGAIN: only one command per
line as the rest of the line is ignored. You can put in comments as many as
you  like  there  is  no need to start them with a ; or /* as everything is
ignored that is not a command or ANSI-Code.

I  put  in  a default for generating a list so you do not need to specify a
config  file.  As  this default list is very simple you really should use a
config file.

-O<outputfile>
--------------

This  option  specifies  the  path  and  name  you  want to use to save the
generated list. Use the full path to be sure it will be where you need it.

Default is RAM:Userlist

-S<sortierkriterium>
--------------------

<sortierkriterium> is a number between 1 and 22. Every number specifys an
other way of sorting the list. The numbers start following sortactions:
 1 first name
 2 last name
 3 street address
 4 city
 5 state
 6 phone number
 7 computer
 8 birth date
 9 postal code
10 handle
11 last call
12 downloaded kbyte
13 downloaded files
14 uploaded kbyte
15 uploaded files
16 written mesages
17 calls
18 upload:download ratio
19 dayly timelimit
20 access
21 netmailaccess
22 usernumber

Default is 2.

-U<userverzeichnis>
-------------------

<userverzeichnis>  is the directory where your userfiles are stored. If you
set  up  TransAmiga as suggested in the manual it is BBS:Users. The default
is BBS:Users

-L<leaveoutfile>
----------------

<leaveoutfile>  this  is the path and name of a special file. Every line of
this  file  is  taken as one filename (NO PATH NEEDED). The filenames taken
from  this  leaveoutfile  tell userlist what files should not be processed.
You can put in here Users.BBS as this is a file that is stored in BBS:Users
but  is not a user file. As this file is always there I included a check so
you need no leaveoutfile if there is only a Users.BBS in there. You can use
this  file  to  take some users out of the list. Just write their name into
the  file  and  you  never will see them again in your list. This is only a
side  effect I first did not think about... The main reason I included this
is  that  you  can stop userlist from processing files like usersfiles that
aren't  userfiles at all. Got me? If you have more files like the Users.BBS
in  your  BBS:Users  you  can  prevent  userlist  from  processing them and
displaying  wired  things  in  the userlist.... I hope everyone understands
what I mean. Just experiment a little with this option and you will get me.



                          Supplied ARexx-Scripts
                          ======================
                          ======================

All ARexx-scripts are examples! They are ment as a basic thing you edit to
your  needs.  You can add a search option or something else. I was too busy
to add that this time. Perhaps a future release will have this feature.

Userlist.trans
--------------

This  is  an  English  version. It calls userlist to generate the lists and
then displays them. The speed mainly depends on the speed of your drive and
the  number  of  your  users.  Don't  forget  to  edit  it  if  you  change
userlist.cfg.

Userlist2.trans
---------------

This is also an English version. This script is made for those who generate
the  lists  in  an  event  every  day.  The  AmigaDOS script Userlistscript
generates the lists you need for this ARexx-script. If you want to use this
script  just  rename  it to userlist.trans and copy it to BBS:Rexx. This is
the suggested solution as it is the fastest.

UserlistGER.trans
-----------------

The  same  as Userlist.trans only in German. Rename it to Userlist.trans if
you want to use it.

Userlist2GER.trans
------------------

Again  a  German  version,  this  time  of  Userlist2.trans.  Rename  it to
Userlist.trans and copy it to BBS:Rexx to use it.

Future:
=======

Perhaps  a  faster  version,  not  sure about this as I optimized heaviely.
Perhaps  I'll  find  something :-) The speed mostly depends on the speed of
your drive and the number of your users.
Perhals generating ANSI and ASCII versions at the same time. It is possible
and  not  too  dificult to implement. If some people ask for this feature I
will implement it :-)
A switch to reverse sorttype. e.g. if you sort by usernumber usually from 1
to  the  highest  number  the  switch  indicates  that you want sorted from
highest  usernumber  to  lowest.  You  can  generate TopTens, TopTewntys or
TopTotals in an easy way...
